## Basic Commands

Stop both trains before each demo:

    tr 14 0
    tr 13 0

Show tracked TC2 train states and current destinations:

    trains

Give a train a destination:

    tc2dest <train> <destination> <speed>

Example:

    tc2dest 14 D10 120

Supported calibrated destinations:

    A5
    B7
    C8
    D10
    LOOP

Sensor attribution test:

    tc2sensor <sensor>

Examples:

    tc2sensor A5
    tc2sensor BAD

Expected sensor reports are attributed to the active train when possible. Unexpected reports are treated as spurious.

## TC2 Demo Commands

The following four demo commands were calibrated and tested on Track D using trains 13 and 14.

### Demo 1: B7 / LOOP catch-up hold

Command:

    tc2b7loop

Equivalent tuning command:

    tc2b7loopx 4 2 30 46 17

Scenario:

Train 14 goes to B7. Train 13 goes to LOOP. Train 13 starts behind train 14 and catches up near the shared section around switch 8. Train 13 waits until train 14 clears the shared section. Then switch 8 is released to the LOOP route and train 13 continues to LOOP.

This demonstrates simultaneous train movement, shared-route reservation, waiting, route handoff, and collision avoidance.

### Demo 2: B7 / D10 crossing wait

Command:

    tc2b7d10

Equivalent tuning command:

    tc2b7d10x 4 54 56 76 40

Scenario:

Train 14 goes to B7. Train 13 goes to D10. Train 13 moves to a safe hold point between switch 8 and switch 17. Train 13 waits while train 14 finishes its route to B7. After train 14 reaches B7, the downstream route is released for D10 and train 13 continues to D10.

This demonstrates simultaneous train movement, a mid-route hold point, time-domain collision avoidance, and safe turnout release.

### Demo 3: D10 / A5 no-wait case

Command:

    tc2d10a5

Equivalent tuning command:

    tc2d10a5x 4 20 17

Scenario:

Train 14 goes to D10. Train 13 goes to A5. Both trains run at speed 120. The calibrated timing is safe, so no waiting is needed. Train 13 stops at A5 and train 14 stops at D10.

This demonstrates that the controller does not unnecessarily stop trains when the route timing is safe.

### Demo 4: Collision avoidance stop

Command:

    tc2collision

Scenario:

Train 14 is assigned toward D10 and runs at speed 120. Train 13 is assigned toward A5 and runs at speed 100. The calibrated collision profile uses gap 4, train 14 total ticks 28, and train 13 total ticks 24. This timing profile is treated as a shared-track collision risk. The controller prevents the unsafe continuation by stopping both trains safely.

This demonstrates that two trains attempting to use an unsafe shared-track timing are prevented from continuing.

## Route Model

The implementation uses calibrated named routes. Each named destination has a route consisting of turnout settings. The controller applies the turnout settings, runs the train for a calibrated number of ticks, and then stops the train with speed 0.

The route table uses physical track diagram switch labels. The CAN switch layer maps those physical labels to the observed CAN accessory address internally.

Calibrated route settings:

    A5 route:    8 S
    B7 route:    8 S, 17 S, 155 S
    C8 route:    8 S, 17 S, 156 S, 15 S
    D10 route:   8 S, 17 S, 156 S
    LOOP internally.

Calibrated route settings:

    A5 route:    8 S
    B7 route:    8 S, 17 S, 155 S
    C route:  8 C, 7 S, 18 C, 3 S, 2 C, 1 C

Calibrated speed-120 stopping data from TC1:

